  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nToday through Sunday: Scattered, Light Rain Showers - Drier Sunday\n\nThe forecast for the rest of today is neither frightful nor \nscary, given that current observations early this afternoon \ncontinues to show isolated to scattered light rain showers \nacross portions of the forecast area. The rain shower activity \nhas been waning over the past few hours and that trend should \nextend into the afternoon through this evening. No impacts are \nexpected due to the light rainfall. As for this evening during \nHalloween activities, the weather should remain delightful with \ntemperatures mainly in the 50s, light wind, and mostly dry \nconditions. Some rain showers may try to redevelop overnight \nacross Minnesota into west central Wisconsin, but if any of that\nactivity does reach the CWA, it should be after midnight. \nOvercast skies are expected to clear out a bit on Sunday to \nallow for stronger daytime heating resulting in afternoon highs \nin the 60s. Dry conditions are currently forecast for Sunday.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nAn upper level trough will move onshore from the Pacific on Sunday\nand will continue its trek east early this next work week. A \nstrong surge of H700 theta-e advection will quickly move \nnorthward out ahead of the trough and is expected to reach the \nUpper Midwest on Monday morning. The lift provided by the trough\nand the plentiful moisture will result in widespread rain \nshowers with embedded thunderstorms on Monday into Tuesday. \nForecast soundings indicate that there may be just enough \ninstability at times throughout the day to develop isolated \nthunderstorms with a threat for lightning. Otherwise, light to \nmoderate rainfall is expected during these two days with the \nresultant rainfall totals having a high chance (+70%) of \nreaching or exceeding 1 inch area wide. The reasonable high end \nrainfall totals are upwards of 2.5-3 inches (10% chance or less \nof occurring) from Monday through Wednesday morning. Even though\nit will be cloudy and rainy during these days, temperatures \nwill more likely than not remain above average.\n\nLate Next Week: Trending Drier with Above Normal Temperatures\n\nBeyond Tuesday, model guidance diverges with the evolution of \nthe trough that provided the rainfall chances earlier in the \nweek. Some guidance results in a cutoff low to the south of the \nforecast area whereas others are more progressive and quickly \nmove the low off to the northeast. The longer the low pressure \nsystem remains in the vicinity, the longer any cooler air will \nstick around on the backside of the system. Right now, NBM \nblended high temperatures for Wednesday through the end of the \nweek remain above normal, but are a bit cooler in the 50s. Any \nrain chances beyond Wednesday morning will be highly dependent \nupon the progression of the upper level low.",
